Card data from Scryfall (https://scryfall.com), used under their API
guidelines. The printed-set catalogs under packages/data/data/ carry card
names, types and rules text from Scryfall. Images are never mirrored:
`@mtg/image-cache` fetches an image once, on demand, keyed by URL, and keeps it
in a gitignored local cache.

Vocabulary data from MTGJSON (https://mtgjson.com), MIT licensed.

Human draft and play data from 17Lands (https://www.17lands.com), CC BY 4.0.
The simulator's replay logs emit a superset of the 17Lands replay schema under
identical column names so that calibration against that data is a join.

Ported and referenced engines
-----------------------------

XMage (https://github.com/magefree/mage) and Argentum
(https://github.com/argentum-mtg/argentum), both MIT licensed, were mined as
reference designs for the continuous-effects and replacement-effect
subsystems. Where a file is a port rather than an independent implementation,
its header names the upstream file and its license, and those headers are
required by this project's engine-strategy decision record rather than
optional courtesy.

Forge (https://github.com/Card-Forge/forge) is GPL-3.0. Forge itself is never
vendored: it is downloaded into a gitignored directory and run as a separate
process, as the rules engine behind the printed formats and as a conformance
oracle. A continuous-integration guard fails the build if any Forge source,
jar, class file or upstream path fragment becomes tracked in this repository.

Two kinds of Forge-derived material are in this tree and remain GPL-3.0 (see
LICENSE and LICENSE-GPL-3.0): the Java bridge under
packages/forge-export/bridge/, compiled against an installed Forge and run in
its own process, and lines of Forge card script quoted in the play-admission
evidence and the Legacy Cube decision ledger as a record of what a Forge
release does. The rest of the project is AGPL-3.0-or-later, which section 13
of the GPL version 3 permits combining with GPL-3.0 work.

Draftmancer (https://github.com/Draftmancer/Draftmancer, MIT) is the draft
surface `@mtg/draft-export` writes a custom-set file for. Nothing of it is
copied here either; the exporter writes a file that tool can open.

Third-party npm dependencies are listed in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md.
